{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NofqHNNBZi99suiULU1Geg","type":"tool_result","content":"chapter-01-taking-departure.md:436:And throughout, you'll see italic bracketed pointers like *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §3 — StatefulSets and stable identity]*. These are cross-bearings: forward and back references to where a concept gets its full treatment. Follow them or don't; they're there so you're never stuck wondering \"did the book cover this?\"\nchapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md:600:That is not a stray observation; it is the spine of the book's closing argument. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 9 §1 — CNI and pod networking]* *[cross-bearing: see Ch 11 §2 — CSI and storage drivers]* *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §3 — CRDs and extending the API]* *[cross-bearing: see Ch 17 §4 — the four pluggable interfaces, collected]*\nchapter-04-records-of-intent.md:207:[Omitted long matching line]\nchapter-04-records-of-intent.md:269:*(Deployment is Chapter 6's resource and the instances it manages are Chapter 5's. Take the example for its shape — a number in a spec, a reality in a status, a correction — and meet the resource properly later. [cross-bearing: see Ch 6 — Deployments and ReplicaSets])*\nchapter-04-records-of-intent.md:688:[Omitted long matching line]\nchapter-03-the-ship-s-company.md:831:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 — ReplicaSet, a control loop you can watch working in real time]*\nchapter-03-the-ship-s-company.md:956:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 — controllers you configure yourself]*\nchapter-05-the-smallest-vessel.md:539:> 🪝 **Snag:** A Pod on a failed node is **not rescheduled onto a healthy node.** It is deleted, and something creates a new one. The word \"rescheduled\" implies the same object moving, which will lead you to the wrong answer on questions about UIDs, about identity, and about why StatefulSets are different *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 — StatefulSets]*. Kubernetes does not move Pods. It replaces them.\nchapter-05-the-smallest-vessel.md:551:That's Chapter 6, and Chapter 4 already pointed you at it *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §1 — the resource that holds the surviving intent]*.\nchapter-05-the-smallest-vessel.md:858:[Omitted long matching line]\nchapter-05-the-smallest-vessel.md:1464:Chapter 5 introduced the disposable thing. Chapter 6 introduces what holds the intent *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §1 — Deployments, ReplicaSets, and the Pod template]*. It's where the shape of every real Kubernetes workload finally appears, and where you'll find out that the Pod you spent this chapter learning is something you will almost never create directly."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"452dc64f-97ea-42e9-8053-7210317581e0","uuid":"0acb8065-a664-4585-9472-549962a31b36","timestamp":"2026-08-24T12:58:59.206Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"chapter-01-taking-departure.md:436:And throughout, you'll see italic bracketed pointers like *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §3 — StatefulSets and stable identity]*. These are cross-bearings: forward and back references to where a concept gets its full treatment. Follow them or don't; they're there so you're never stuck wondering \"did the book cover this?\"\nchapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md:600:That is not a stray observation; it is the spine of the book's closing argument. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 9 §1 — CNI and pod networking]* *[cross-bearing: see Ch 11 §2 — CSI and storage drivers]* *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §3 — CRDs and extending the API]* *[cross-bearing: see Ch 17 §4 — the four pluggable interfaces, collected]*\nchapter-04-records-of-intent.md:207:[Omitted long matching line]\nchapter-04-records-of-intent.md:269:*(Deployment is Chapter 6's resource and the instances it manages are Chapter 5's. Take the example for its shape — a number in a spec, a reality in a status, a correction — and meet the resource properly later. [cross-bearing: see Ch 6 — Deployments and ReplicaSets])*\nchapter-04-records-of-intent.md:688:[Omitted long matching line]\nchapter-03-the-ship-s-company.md:831:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 — ReplicaSet, a control loop you can watch working in real time]*\nchapter-03-the-ship-s-company.md:956:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 — controllers you configure yourself]*\nchapter-05-the-smallest-vessel.md:539:> 🪝 **Snag:** A Pod on a failed node is **not rescheduled onto a healthy node.** It is deleted, and something creates a new one. The word \"rescheduled\" implies the same object moving, which will lead you to the wrong answer on questions about UIDs, about identity, and about why StatefulSets are different *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 — StatefulSets]*. Kubernetes does not move Pods. It replaces them.\nchapter-05-the-smallest-vessel.md:551:That's Chapter 6, and Chapter 4 already pointed you at it *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §1 — the resource that holds the surviving intent]*.\nchapter-05-the-smallest-vessel.md:858:[Omitted long matching line]\nchapter-05-the-smallest-vessel.md:1464:Chapter 5 introduced the disposable thing. Chapter 6 introduces what holds the intent *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §1 — Deployments, ReplicaSets, and the Pod template]*. It's where the shape of every real Kubernetes workload finally appears, and where you'll find out that the Pod you spent this chapter learning is something you will almost never create directly.","numLines":11,"totalLines":11}}
